---
import Architecture from '../../layouts/Architecture.astro';
import Diagram from '../../components/arch/Diagram.astro';
import Box from '../../components/arch/Box.astro';
import Edge from '../../components/arch/Edge.astro';
import Group from '../../components/arch/Group.astro';
import Label from '../../components/arch/Label.astro';
import Legend from '../../components/arch/Legend.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/arch/Callout.astro';

// Entry-point columns: 3 columns, 190 wide, 16 gap, after an 80 unit row-label gutter.
const cx = (c: number) => 92 + c * 206;
const CW = 190;
const rows = { name: 20, before: 100, side: 198, protect: 296, route: 394 };
---
<Architecture
  title="Skipping"
  subtitle="What a button press does to reels that are still spinning: slam, quicken, protection and groups."
  eyebrow="Presses"
>
  <p>
    Players tap the spin button while the reels are still moving. Some want the
    columns to slam down right now and show the result. Some want them to
    hurry, but still land with the little bounce. A game that is teasing a
    big win wants the first tap to drop the boring columns and leave the two
    teasing ones crawling. And a game with reel groups wants each tap to drop
    the next group, not everything. All of those are "a skip", and the engine
    has to make them feel different while staying correct about what has
    landed and what has not.
  </p>

  <p>
    pixi-reels splits the problem in two. The <strong>mode</strong> says how a
    freed reel lands: a <strong>slam</strong> puts it on its result frame and
    lands it in the same tick, while a <strong>quicken</strong> ends the tease
    and cuts the stop delay but still plays the spin-out and the bounce. The
    <strong>group</strong> says which reels a press frees, and that part is
    decided from reel groups and tease protection, never from the mode. Three
    public methods feed a press into that machinery, and the rest of this page
    follows a press through it.
  </p>

  <Callout kind="plain">
    A press answers two questions, in this order: which reels are freed by
    this tap, and how do they land. Groups and protection answer the first.
    The mode answers the second. Keeping them apart is what lets a protected
    tease survive a slam and lets a quicken respect groups.
  </Callout>

  <h2>The three entry points</h2>

  <p>
    Figure 1 compares the three methods your game can call, one per column,
    with the four questions that separate them as rows. Read a column top to
    bottom. Only <code>skip()</code> carries the once-per-round side effect
    (the speed boost); only <code>requestSkip()</code> may be called before
    the result is in; only <code>slamStop()</code> walks past tease
    protection. Every column ends in the same two internal routines.
  </p>

  <Diagram height={492} caption="The three public entry points, row by row. Only skip() carries the once-per-round side effect; only requestSkip() may be called before the result; only slamStop() walks past tease protection. Every column ends in _slamTargets or _quicken.">
    <Label x={16} y={rows.before + 34} text={['before', 'setResult']} mono />
    <Label x={16} y={rows.side + 34} text={['side', 'effect']} mono />
    <Label x={16} y={rows.protect + 34} text={['tease', 'protect']} mono />
    <Label x={16} y={rows.route + 34} text={['routes', 'through']} mono />

    <Box x={cx(0)} y={rows.name} w={CW} h={60} title="skip(options?)" kind="primary" sub="ReelSet.skipSpin()" />
    <Box x={cx(1)} y={rows.name} w={CW} h={60} title="requestSkip(opts?)" kind="primary" sub="ReelSet.requestSkip()" />
    <Box x={cx(2)} y={rows.name} w={CW} h={60} title="slamStop(opts?)" kind="primary" sub="{ reels } or { except }" />

    <Box x={cx(0)} y={rows.before} w={CW} h={78} title="throws" mono={false} kind="muted" sub={['the round is not', 'decided yet']} />
    <Box x={cx(1)} y={rows.before} w={CW} h={78} title="queues" mono={false} kind="accent" sub={['_skipPending, skip:queued,', 'fires inside setResult']} />
    <Box x={cx(2)} y={rows.before} w={CW} h={78} title="throws" mono={false} kind="muted" sub={['no result frame', 'to place']} />

    <Box x={cx(0)} y={rows.side} w={CW} h={78} title="once per round" mono={false} kind="accent" sub={['standard: speed boost', 'cascade: auto-slam refills']} />
    <Box x={cx(1)} y={rows.side} w={CW} h={78} title="none" mono={false} kind="muted" sub={['presses with', 'withSideEffects = false']} />
    <Box x={cx(2)} y={rows.side} w={CW} h={78} title="none" mono={false} kind="muted" sub={['skipStage only', 'is touched']} />

    <Box x={cx(0)} y={rows.protect} w={CW} h={78} title="honoured" mono={false} kind="accent" sub={['protected tease reels', 'stay up']} />
    <Box x={cx(1)} y={rows.protect} w={CW} h={78} title="honoured" mono={false} kind="accent" sub={['sees protection set', 'before setResult']} />
    <Box x={cx(2)} y={rows.protect} w={CW} h={78} title="ignored" mono={false} kind="muted" sub={['also ignores', 'defaultSkipMode']} />

    <Box x={cx(0)} y={rows.route} w={CW} h={78} title="_pressSkip(true)" kind="subtle" sub={['carried across', 'expand() steps']} />
    <Box x={cx(1)} y={rows.route} w={CW} h={78} title="_pressSkip(false)" kind="subtle" sub={['skipStage waits', 'for the fire']} />
    <Box x={cx(2)} y={rows.route} w={CW} h={78} title="_slamWithStage" kind="subtle" sub={['no arg: stage 2, full slam', 'reels/except: partial']} />

    {[0, 1, 2].map((c) => (
      <>
        <Edge from={[cx(c) + CW / 2, rows.name + 60]} to={[cx(c) + CW / 2, rows.before]} arrow="none" style="dotted" />
        <Edge from={[cx(c) + CW / 2, rows.before + 78]} to={[cx(c) + CW / 2, rows.side]} arrow="none" style="dotted" />
        <Edge from={[cx(c) + CW / 2, rows.side + 78]} to={[cx(c) + CW / 2, rows.protect]} arrow="none" style="dotted" />
        <Edge from={[cx(c) + CW / 2, rows.protect + 78]} to={[cx(c) + CW / 2, rows.route]} />
      </>
    ))}
  </Diagram>

  <div class="not-prose my-8">
    <Legend items={[
      { kind: 'primary', label: 'public entry', note: 'what your game calls' },
      { kind: 'accent', label: 'has a consequence', note: 'a queue, a side effect, or an honoured protection' },
      { kind: 'muted', label: 'inert', note: 'throws or does nothing on that row' },
      { kind: 'subtle', label: 'internal method', note: 'where the press goes next' },
    ]} />
  </div>

  <p>
    <strong><code>skip()</code></strong> is the ordinary player press: it
    throws before the result is in, honours protection, and the first press
    that ends a round applies the round's side effect (below).
    <strong><code>requestSkip()</code></strong> is the same press for a game
    that lets the player tap before the server has answered: it queues the
    press, emits <code>skip:queued</code>, and fires it from inside
    <code>setResult()</code>, with no side effect.
    <strong><code>slamStop()</code></strong> is the hammer for your own
    features: it ignores protection and the default mode, slams only
    <code>{'{ reels }'}</code> or all but <code>{'{ except }'}</code>, and with
    no argument is a full slam that ends the round.
  </p>

  <p>
    There is a fourth path your game never calls. <code>abortSlam()</code> is
    what the abort signal, the timeout and the error recovery use when
    something has gone wrong and the board must be left tidy. It ignores
    protection and the default mode, has no side effect, and lands whatever
    the reels happen to be showing (halt and snap, no result frame needed).
    It goes through the slam routine with every reel, so it bumps the
    generation counter and ends every chain.
  </p>

  <h2>The options</h2>

  <p>The option and payload types, listed once.</p>
  <div class="not-prose my-6">
    <div class="rounded-xl border border-border bg-card overflow-hidden">
      <table class="w-full text-sm">
        <thead class="bg-muted/60">
          <tr>
            <th class="text-left px-4 py-2 font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wider text-muted-foreground">Type</th>
            <th class="text-left px-4 py-2 font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wider text-muted-foreground">Shape</th>
            <th class="text-left px-4 py-2 font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wider text-muted-foreground">Notes</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {[
            ['SkipMode', "'slam' | 'quicken'", "the default is defaultSkipMode on the controller, set by the builder's skipMode(mode), else 'slam'"],
            ['SkipOptions', '{ mode?, speed?, payload? }', 'speed names a registered profile a quicken finishes on (its spinSpeed drives the spin-out, its bounce the landing); unknown names throw, a slam ignores it. payload passes through untouched to onSkip, the skip events and SpinResult.skipContext'],
            ['SkipContext', '{ mode, speed?, payload? }', "what every phase's onSkip receives; engine slams use { mode: 'slam' }"],
            ['SkipInfo', 'SkipContext & { reels: number[], partial: boolean }', 'the payload of skip:requested and skip:completed'],
            ['SlamOptions', '{ reels? } | { except? }', 'one or the other, not both. Out-of-range indices are ignored; held and already-landed reels are never slammed'],
          ].map(([k, v, n]) => (
            <tr class="border-t border-border/60">
              <td class="px-4 py-2.5 font-mono text-primary text-xs whitespace-nowrap">{k}</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-2.5 font-mono text-xs text-muted-foreground">{v}</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-xs text-foreground/80">{n}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p>
    There is no separate "hurry up" method. "Finish faster but keep the
    landing" is <code>requestSkip({'{ mode: \'quicken\' }'})</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Which reels a press frees</h2>

  <p>
    Every press that honours protection, whether it came from
    <code>skip()</code>, from a queued <code>requestSkip()</code> firing inside
    <code>setResult()</code>, or carried over to the next <code>expand()</code>
    step, goes through the same decision. Figure 2 is that decision. Start at
    the top and follow the arrows. Groups win over protection; protection only
    matters while there is a tease worth protecting; and once only tease reels
    are left, the protect mode decides whether a press releases one of them,
    or nothing at all.
  </p>

  <Diagram height={544} caption="_pressSkip asks _nextSlamGroup which reels a press frees. Groups win over protection; protection only matters while there is an effective hold; and once only tease reels are left, 'stepwise' releases them one by one while 'once' and 'always' free nothing.">
    <Box x={200} y={20} w={230} h={60} title="press" mono={false} kind="primary" sub="skip() or queued requestSkip()" />

    <Box x={200} y={130} w={230} h={60} title="reel groups set?" mono={false} sub="setReelGroups()" />
    <Box x={500} y={121} w={200} h={78} title="next unlanded group" mono={false} kind="accent" sub={['non-teasing reels first,', 'then its tease reels']} />
    <Edge from={[315, 80]} to={[315, 130]} />
    <Edge from={[430, 160]} to={[500, 160]} label="yes" />

    <Box x={200} y={240} w={230} h={60} title="protected tease reels?" mono={false} sub="_protectedTeaseReels" />
    <Box x={500} y={231} w={200} h={78} title="full slam / quicken" mono={false} kind="accent" sub={['every reel still up,', 'stage 2, side effect now']} />
    <Edge from={[315, 190]} to={[315, 240]} label="no" labelDx={36} />
    <Edge from={[430, 270]} to={[500, 270]} label="no" />

    <Box x={200} y={350} w={230} h={60} title="non-tease reels still up?" mono={false} />
    <Box x={500} y={341} w={200} h={78} title="all but protected" mono={false} kind="accent" sub={['stage 1, side effect', 'waits for the next press']} />
    <Edge from={[315, 300]} to={[315, 350]} label="yes" labelDx={40} />
    <Edge from={[430, 380]} to={[500, 380]} label="yes" />

    <Box x={200} y={460} w={230} h={60} title="only tease reels left" mono={false} kind="muted" sub="which protect mode?" />
    <Edge from={[315, 410]} to={[315, 460]} label="no" labelDx={36} />
    <Box x={20} y={451} w={130} h={78} title="'stepwise'" kind="accent" sub={['one tease reel', 'per press']} />
    <Box x={500} y={451} w={200} h={78} title="'once' / 'always'" kind="muted" sub={['empty group:', 'the press does nothing']} />
    <Edge from={[200, 490]} to={[150, 490]} />
    <Edge from={[430, 490]} to={[500, 490]} />
  </Diagram>

  <p>
    "Protected tease reels" is empty, so the press falls through to a full
    slam, whenever <code>protect</code> is false, <code>'once'</code> is
    already spent, the effective hold is 0 or less (read from the profile
    captured for this spin), or every tease reel has landed. The
    <strong>skip stage</strong>, readable as <code>skipStage</code>, is the
    controller's memory of the round: 0 at the boundary, 1 when a press left
    something spinning (the side effect is still owed), 2 when a press ended
    the round and the side effect has been applied.
  </p>

  <p>
    Two details the picture leaves out. A quicken press counts the reels it
    already quickened as down, so the next press moves on to the next group.
    And with groups set a press releases only the next unlanded group: with
    <code>[[0, 1], [2, 3], [4]]</code> the player needs three presses.
  </p>

  <h2>Slam versus quicken, on one stop phase</h2>

  <p>
    The clearest way to feel the difference between the two modes is to watch
    both hit the same phase at the same moment. Figure 3 is a slam arriving
    while a stop phase is in its spin-out step. The running step is cancelled
    and the rest are dropped; the phase's skip handler sets the final pose
    itself, and the reel is landed before the frame ends. The player sees the
    column snap.
  </p>

  <Diagram height={358} caption="A slam on a stop phase that is in its spinOut step. The running step is cancelled (phase:step cancelled), the rest are dropped, and onSkip sets the final pose in the same tick.">
    <Group x={20} y={20} w={680} h={318} kind="tint" label="slam" note="forceComplete(ctx)" />
    {(['delay', 'spinOut', 'land', 'bounce'] as const).map((s, i) => (
      <Box x={44 + i * 162} y={60} w={146} h={44} title={s} kind={s === 'spinOut' ? 'accent' : 'ghost'} />
    ))}
    {[0, 1, 2].map((i) => (
      <Edge from={[44 + i * 162 + 146, 82]} to={[44 + (i + 1) * 162, 82]} arrow="none" style="dotted" />
    ))}
    <Edge from={[279, 104]} to={[279, 150]} kind="primary" />
    <Box x={160} y={150} w={400} h={78} title="StopPhase.onSkip" kind="primary" sub={['haltDrive, placeStrip(targetFrame),', 'container back to _baseY, snapToGrid']} />
    <Edge from={[360, 228]} to={[360, 270]} kind="primary" />
    <Box x={240} y={270} w={240} h={44} title="landed, same tick" mono={false} kind="accent" />
  </Diagram>

  <p>
    Figure 4 is a quicken arriving at the same phase. Only the delay step is a
    cut step, so it is the only thing skipped or cut short. The spin-out, the
    landing and the bounce all still play, optionally on a different speed
    profile named in the press. The player sees the column hurry, then land
    properly.
  </p>

  <Diagram height={358} caption="A quicken on the same stop phase. Only the cut step, delay, is skipped or cut short; spinOut, land and bounce still play, optionally on another speed profile named in the press.">
    <Group x={20} y={20} w={680} h={318} kind="tint" label="quicken" note="phase.skip({ mode: 'quicken', speed? })" />
    <Box x={44} y={60} w={146} h={44} title="delay" kind="muted" />
    <Box x={206} y={60} w={146} h={44} title="spinOut" kind="accent" />
    <Box x={368} y={60} w={146} h={44} title="land" kind="accent" />
    <Box x={530} y={60} w={146} h={44} title="bounce" kind="accent" />
    {[0, 1, 2].map((i) => (
      <Edge from={[44 + i * 162 + 146, 82]} to={[44 + (i + 1) * 162, 82]} />
    ))}
    <Edge from={[279, 104]} to={[279, 150]} kind="primary" />
    <Box x={160} y={150} w={400} h={78} title="ctx.speed (optional)" kind="subtle" sub={['its spinSpeed drives the spin-out,', 'its bounce settings drive the landing']} />
    <Edge from={[360, 228]} to={[360, 270]} kind="primary" />
    <Box x={240} y={270} w={240} h={44} title="lands after the spin-out" mono={false} kind="accent" />
  </Diagram>

  <p>
    Here is what the <strong>slam routine</strong> does with the set of freed
    reels. It emits <code>skip:requested</code> with the mode, the reels and
    whether the slam is partial. For a partial slam it force-completes each
    target's active phase and adds the reel to the slammed set; for a full slam
    it force-completes every phase and bumps the generation counter. With a
    result in hand it applies any pending MultiWays reshape, then for each reel
    places the symbols straight onto the strip and raises the reel's landing
    events, which is where <code>spin:reelLanding</code> comes from, and marks
    it landed. Without a result (the abort case) it halts, snaps and marks. It
    emits <code>skip:completed</code>. After a partial slam it re-runs the
    all-started check and the stop gate, because a reel that was slammed
    before it ever reached its spin phase would otherwise leave the gate closed
    for good.
  </p>

  <p>
    The <strong>quicken routine</strong> is lighter because the reels keep
    running. It marks the targets as quickened, sets each reel's reference
    speed from the named profile if there was one, records the batch, emits
    <code>skip:requested</code>, and calls <code>skip()</code> on each target's
    active phase. A phase that is not quickenable keeps running and the chain
    applies the quicken at its next decision point: the spin phase drops its
    minimum to 0, a tease phase created after the press starts already
    quickened, and the stop phase gets no delay and no
    <code>preserveSpeed</code>. <code>skip:completed</code> fires later, when
    the batch's last reel lands. In cascade mode a quicken becomes a slam with
    a one-time <code>quicken-cascade</code> warning, because the cascade
    phases' slam pose is already their natural end.
  </p>

  <Callout kind="why">
    A slam has to be a cancel, not a fast-forward. The stop phase learned this
    the hard way: snapping to the grid in the middle of a bounce used to lock
    in the overshoot, which is why its slam path first resets the container to
    its home position and only then snaps. So a slam cancels whatever is
    running and sets the final pose directly, and the quicken exists for the
    cases where you do want the landing to play.
  </Callout>

  <h2>The generation counter and partial slams</h2>

  <p>
    Every reel chain remembers the <strong>generation</strong>, a counter the
    controller bumps when a round ends or restarts, and checks it after every
    await: if the number has moved on, the chain stops quietly. A full slam
    bumps it, which is how one call ends five chains. A partial slam cannot,
    because the other reels must keep spinning, so it marks only its targets
    in the slammed set, and the staleness check treats "in that set" like "old
    generation". The watchdog, the abort callbacks and the per-chain safety
    net check the generation too, so late errors from an older spin are
    dropped. After a partial slam the skip stage is left alone, unless the
    slammed set covered every reel still spinning, in which case the round is
    over and the stage moves to 2.
  </p>

  <h2>Reel groups</h2>

  <p>
    Reel groups turn five independent columns into a few sets that land
    together. <code>setReelGroups(groups)</code> must list every reel exactly
    once or it throws, and it throws if any reel has already landed in the
    current round; <code>null</code> clears them. Groups persist across spins.
    When stopping, a reel waits until every earlier group has landed, and its
    stop delay becomes its position within the group times the profile's
    <code>stopDelay</code>. When skipping, a press releases the next group that
    has not landed, non-teasing reels first. If reels are added later they are
    appended as one trailing group; removed reels are filtered out, and the
    groups are cleared if nothing is left.
  </p>

  <h2>Protect modes</h2>

  <p>
    Protection is set on the tease, through <code>setAnticipation</code>, and
    says what a player press may end. The table reads left to right: what the
    first press does, then what later presses do.
  </p>
  <div class="not-prose my-6">
    <div class="rounded-xl border border-border bg-card overflow-hidden">
      <table class="w-full text-sm">
        <thead class="bg-muted/60">
          <tr>
            <th class="text-left px-4 py-2 font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wider text-muted-foreground">protect</th>
            <th class="text-left px-4 py-2 font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wider text-muted-foreground">First press</th>
            <th class="text-left px-4 py-2 font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wider text-muted-foreground">Later presses</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {[
            ['false (default)', 'full slam, stage 2', 'nothing left to press'],
            ["'once' (or true)", 'lands the non-tease reels, stage 1', 'protection is spent: the next press slams the tease reels and applies the side effect'],
            ["'stepwise'", 'lands the non-tease reels, stage 1', 'each press releases the next tease reel in tease order; stage 2 on the last'],
            ["'always'", 'lands the non-tease reels, stage 1', 'a press never ends a tease; stage stays at 1 until the round ends on its own'],
          ].map(([m, a, b]) => (
            <tr class="border-t border-border/60">
              <td class="px-4 py-2.5 font-mono text-primary text-xs whitespace-nowrap">{m}</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-xs text-foreground/80">{a}</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-xs text-foreground/80">{b}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p>
    Pass <code>protect: true</code> and it is normalised to <code>'once'</code>
    inside <code>setAnticipation</code>; storing the raw boolean used to behave
    like <code>'always'</code>. Protection applies to <code>skip()</code> and
    <code>requestSkip()</code> only, never to <code>slamStop()</code>. It is
    inert when the effective hold is 0 or less, and the hold is read from the
    profile captured for this spin, not the live one: on TURBO without a
    <code>duration</code> override the 250 ms hold keeps it active, on
    SUPER_TURBO it does nothing. The tease itself is described on the
    <a href="/architecture/anticipation/">anticipation page</a>.
  </p>

  <Callout kind="gotcha">
    With <code>protect: 'always'</code> the skip stage never reaches 2, because
    no press is allowed to end the round. If your game uses the stage to decide
    whether a round is over, check <code>isSpinning</code> first and treat the
    stage as a hint about presses, not about the round.
  </Callout>

  <h2>Across rounds and expand steps</h2>

  <p>
    The first press that ends a round has a <strong>side effect</strong>, the
    one thing that outlives the spin. In standard mode the engine boosts to
    the registered profile with the highest <code>spinSpeed</code> and emits
    <code>skip:boosted {'{ previous, current }'}</code>, so the next spin is
    faster for a player who keeps tapping. In cascade mode it sets an
    auto-slam flag instead, so every later <code>refill()</code> in the round
    slams at once (each refill's <code>setResult</code> presses a
    side-effect-free slam). The boost calls the speed manager directly, which
    is why it emits <code>skip:boosted</code> but not <code>speed:changed</code>;
    the next <code>spin()</code> silently restores the previous profile unless
    you called <code>setSpeed()</code> in between.
  </p>

  <p>
    A press can also be <strong>carried</strong>: between two
    <code>expand()</code> steps the controller stores it, emits
    <code>skip:queued</code>, and the next step's <code>spin()</code> (with
    <code>continueRound</code>) turns it into a pending press without resetting
    the skip stage. For completeness: a phase chain that throws is slammed by
    the safety net and resolves with <code>wasSkipped: true</code>; an abort or
    timeout goes through <code>abortSlam()</code> and rejects. <code>nudge</code>
    lives on the set, throws while a spin runs, and is untouched by any skip;
    <code>skipNudge()</code> is its own thing.
  </p>

  <h2>Events a press emits</h2>

  <p>
    What your game hears when a press goes through, in the order it happens.
  </p>
  <div class="not-prose my-6">
    <div class="rounded-xl border border-border bg-card overflow-hidden">
      <table class="w-full text-sm">
        <thead class="bg-muted/60">
          <tr>
            <th class="text-left px-4 py-2 font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wider text-muted-foreground">Event</th>
